A||||8||deceler8||Resolving resource conflicts in Win95||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||12-11-2001||01:40 PM||||My Pentium II, 266 meg computer with WIN95 has "hardware conflicts" that I'm unable to resolve. Is there a program that will detect and automatically correct these conflicts ?<P>My main problem is that I have a sound card that won't operate due to these conflicts.<P>If there isn't a program to fix this, how do I go about doing this myself ?<P>I've removed the offending items in Control panel, restarted, removed the actual cards from the computer and reinstalled...you name it, I've probably done it.<P>Anyone ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||12-11-2001||01:49 PM||||You can manualy change the resources in control panel \ system<BR><B>The easiest way to fix it </B><BR>If the sound card is a PNP then just open the box and take the card out and put it back in in a different slot. <P>win98 is stupid (dont worry this is just technical stuff) and will not detect the card again in the same slot. <P><B>alternate way a bit harder for nontechnical types </B><BR>Another trick you can do id go into the CMOS settings and change from PNP OS to disabled or no <P>Edited because I noticed you have 95 which is much dumber than 98 but the above mentioned will work.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||deceler8||12-11-2001||02:02 PM||||Actually, Bill, the sound card is an onboard sound card (this is an IBM 300XL) with a Crystal CS4236B chip.<P>I just recently got this PC, and the sound card worked when I got it, though it was garbled. Now the PC makes an "engine idling" sound with the speakers on.<P>Any of this make any sense ?||||reg||1
Z||000003||thomas_l||12-11-2001||02:10 PM||||Barring hardware that is actually broken, you probably have an addressing or IRQ conflict.<P>Right click on "My Computer" and select "properties". Click on the hardware tab and look for yellow bangs (the "!" character in a yellow caution triangle). That will indicate a conflict, either with memory addressing or IRQ. Go into the advanced properties and see where the conflict is. Usually if there is a conflict, Windows will tell you what other device is stomping on the other. Then you change one or the other to resolve the conflict (or disable one if appropriate).||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||12-11-2001||02:20 PM||||Hate to sound like a doofus, but change them how ?||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||12-11-2001||02:32 PM||||Hehe just opern control panel and click on the system icon<P>then look for sound and game controllers<BR>The list should be expanded with an ! next to it.<P>Doubleclick on the sound card and go to the device settings tab<P>look for IRQ setting and uncheck the automatic box then dbl click on the IRQ to change it<P>Usually it is set to IRQ5 if 10 is available then use it or any other "available" IRQ<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||deceler8||12-11-2001||03:08 PM||||OK...tried to change the resource settings on the device highlighted with the yellow exclamation point...tells me "this resource setting cannot be modified"...<P>What now ?||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||12-11-2001||04:08 PM||||Ok what other device is using the same IRQ? Find it and change it. <P>What IRQ is the sound using?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000008||avengador1||12-11-2001||09:28 PM||||You probably have an isa slot sound card and it is causing a resource conflict. Get a pci slot sound card, that way the resources are shared and you won't have a conflict.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
